AB  - The increasing availability of high-resolution satellite imagery, such as Sentinel-2, provides great potential for land cover classification. However, raw imagery often suffers from issues like cloud cover, haze, and low contrast, reducing classification accuracy. Traditional preprocessing pipelines apply the same techniques to all images, leading to over- or under-processing. This project presents an adaptive preprocessing framework using a shallow multi-label convolutional neural network (CNN) to predict necessary preprocessing operations (e.g., cloud masking, normalization, and contrast enhancement) on a per-patch basis. These steps are applied through a modular pipeline, ensuring tailored enhancements. The cleaned patches are then fed into a U-Net++ segmentation model trained on Dynamic World land cover labels for classes such as trees, grass, crops, water, built-up areas, and bare land. Results demonstrate that adaptive preprocessing improves classification performance, particularly in challenging conditions.
