TI  - A Review on LULC Change Detection Using Machine Learning Algorithms in Google Earth Engine
AU  - Kaur N.
PY  - 2026
T2  - International Conference on Innovative Practices in Technology and Management Iciptm 2026
AB  - Across different geographic regions machine learning algorithms implemented in Google Earth Engine showed the OA (Overall Accuracy) ranging from 80 to 97% for Land Use/Cover change detection. With an accuracy spanning from 90% to 96%, the Random Forest method proved reliable across agricultural, coastal wetland, and mountainous zones, requiring minimal parameter modifications whereas SVM with RBF kernels achieved its peak accuracy of 96.70% but needed extensive parameter adjustments for optimal performance. CART performed particularly well in coastal wetlands, while deep learning models such as Feature Pyramid Networks outperformed traditional methods for rural building detection, although this advantage reduced in complex urban-rural areas. Overall, algorithm performance was strongly dependent on application context, with Sentinel-2 data consistently improving accuracies over Landsat-8, while change class accuracies remained notably lower than stable classes, indicating the greater difficulty of detecting actual land cover changes.
